WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.259
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Jim Freeman, who is author of the book

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:12.100
Rich Thanks to Racism How the Ultra Wealthy
Profit from Racial Injustice, and also director

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.620
of the Social Movement Support Lab at the
University of Denver.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.669
Jim, it's so great to have you on today.

00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:19.929
Speaker 3: Thanks so much for having me, David.

00:00:19.929 --> 00:00:24.739
Speaker 1: So, I mean, to start with, let's
get people into sort of the thesis and idea

00:00:24.739 --> 00:00:29.789
of the book and in what ways can racism be
profitable?

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:32.529
How do people monetize racism?

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:38.040
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, you know, the reason
I wrote the book is I wanted to introduce

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.350
this idea of strategic racism, right.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.440
So particularly after last summer, there's
a lot a lot more understanding of systemic

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.010
racism, which is which is good and exciting.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:57.040
But the problem is that not many people understand
what and who stand in the way of dismantling

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.040
that.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.270
And there's also a problem, too, I think,
with the term, which is just sort of vague

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:03.620
and impersonal.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.670
It makes it seem like systemic racism is this
natural occurrence.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.340
It's just sort of part of our society.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:16.390
So I went and introduced this idea of systemic
racism to demonstrate to people that there

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.040
are people who are profiting from it.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.409
There are people who are racism profiteers
in this country, that there is intentionality

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:27.830
behind these efforts, because even when we
do think that there's people on the other

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:31.260
side of this fight, we think about the right
and those kind of folks.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.410
But that's not who's been passing all these
laws that are responsible for systemic racism,

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.750
that they're not the ones who are doing the
the heavy lifting in defending or expanding

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.350
these policies.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:48.170
So my work as a civil rights lawyer has been
with the communities that are most impacted

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.190
by systemic racism, black and brown communities.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.030
I've been working with those communities for
for 20 years.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:57.500
And so I've seen up close what this looks
like and how devastating it is.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.660
And what that really means in practice is
that these corporate and Wall Street executives,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:06.140
these ultra wealthy billionaires, they make
huge investments in these think tanks and

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:13.270
advocacy organizations and media outlets that
have been instrumental in preserving, defending

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.980
and expanding these dynamics, such as what
we see in the criminal justice system, such

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.900
as what we see in education and immigration.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.740
And and like you say, they are profoundly
profitable.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:28.910
These dynamics for the ultra wealthy, they
make money off of mass incarceration and immigrant

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.570
detention and immigration enforcement and
school privatization.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.890
And they also, you know, they pay less in
taxes when we as a society choose to dehumanize

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.490
people rather than meeting their basic human
needs.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:45.709
And then one thing that became very clear,
I think, to a lot of folks last summer is

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:50.990
that systemic racism, particularly the mass
criminalization and incarceration system that

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:57.590
we have, is extremely effective at controlling
us, at dividing us and causing us not to recognize

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.020
our common interests.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:06.600
And we really saw that in how police were
used to to really stamp down political protests.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.620
So all those dynamics really help maintain
the status quo, help the ultrawealthy maintain

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.230
their political and economic power.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.160
And those are just some of the ways that they
profit from these dynamics.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.920
Speaker 1: Yeah, so and that I mean, that
gives us a good introduction to a lot of different

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.470
industries and corporations that are sort
of directly connected to a lot of these issues.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:31.090
I'd be curious about your thoughts before
we go back to that about I don't know.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.510
I don't want to use the term more like benign
or pedestrian corporations, but just things

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:40.660
more associated with day to day, like, for
example, Starbucks and other companies that

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:46.480
last summer started to make a big show of
we're going to do implicit bias training and

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:52.450
all of our managers and maybe even employees
are going to go through a course and so on

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:53.450
and so forth.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:04:01.550
I've always felt that that was essentially
a PR decision and and no one at its face,

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.019
it didn't really pass the sniff test for me.

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:09.500
But really what got me thinking that was I've
not really found evidence that that type of

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:15.140
training for a company like Starbucks just
to pick one or others, really gets at the

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:18.990
root of some of what is causing these issues
to begin with.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:24.140
So I know that this is kind of a slight deviation
from what you introduced to us.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:29.040
But what's your thought about that as as a
source of profit or at least of positive PR?

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.920
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, you make an excellent
point, which is, you know, last summer we

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.060
had what seems like virtually every corporation
in America coming out with a statement denouncing

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.820
systemic racism.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.080
Yes.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.640
Proclaiming that black lives matter.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.330
And yet here we are nine months later.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:55.110
And what of those corporations done to actually
dismantle these systems?

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.110
Right.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:04.540
To not just like you say, do these sort of
superficial diversity efforts or to donate

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:08.710
some small, tiny percentage of their profits
to black owned businesses?

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:16.990
I'm talking about real efforts to to address
these issues that are harming and even killing

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.540
people in black and brown communities.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.640
They're nowhere to be found.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.259
And in fact, for the most part, they're there.

00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:25.090
They continue to be on the opposite side of
these fights.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.910
So one thing that.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:35.320
Then I talk a lot about is is, you know, those
we've had those types of efforts forever,

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.960
you know, where we we talk about how we're
making progress, right?

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.340
We're taking steps forward.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.889
We've been doing that for over one hundred
years.

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:49.770
I mean, and it's got to be clear by now that,
you know, those things aren't bad persay,

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:55.050
but they're not even close to being enough
and.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:00.110
You know, I think the question for us is how
many more people are we going to allow to

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:01.110
be harmed?

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:05.520
How many more lives are we going to allow
to be taken while we are content not to actually

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.240
solve the problem, but to merely make progress?

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.389
So I think when it comes to racial justice,
we need to remove the word progress from our

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:14.840
vocabulary.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.699
I think it's time to advance real solutions,
but to do so, I think it's going to require

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:24.870
many more people understanding how and why
corporate America and Wall Street are able

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:26.419
to profit from other people's pain.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:29.759
And we're going to need many more white people
in particular to understand how their struggles

00:06:29.759 --> 00:06:33.840
are deeply interconnected with the struggles
of people of color so that they recognize

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.139
that the fight for racial justice is also
their fight.

00:06:36.139 --> 00:06:41.449
Speaker 1: Now, when you talk about that interconnection,
I'm I'm leading here in the sense that I don't

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:46.259
think you're talking about a sort of class
reductionism of just realizing that in the

00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:50.270
same ways that a lot of these racial issues
relate to class.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.880
That can also be a factor for why you're talking
about a different type of interconnection.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.240
Am I right?

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.460
Speaker 3: I am, yeah.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.960
I mean, I think there's there's there's different
types of interconnection.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.449
But but one was really only made apparent
to me when I was researching and writing the

00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:11.669
book, which is as I was diving in the book,
is really an effort to sort of follow the

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:18.050
money behind systemic racism or I call strategic
racism to understand exactly where it's coming

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.490
from and the ideology behind it.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:28.980
But as I was doing that, it was really Eye-Opening
for me, because, you know, as I as I was learning

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.199
more about where this money was coming and
how strategic these investments were, I also

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:37.290
realized how heavily invested the ultra wealthy
are in pushing a political agenda that's been

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.569
deeply harmful to white Americans as well.

00:07:40.569 --> 00:07:43.150
Not in the same ways, of course, and not always
at the same levels.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.330
Frequently not.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:50.930
But but what I say is that if white people
examine the reasons that their lives are far

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.220
more difficult than they need to be, they
will likely eventually run into the same set

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:59.560
of organizations and individuals who are leading
the opposition against racial equality.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.560
Yeah.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.349
So I, for one, was astonished to discover
just how enormous an influence the ultra wealthy

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:10.340
have on my life and on every other white person
I know.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:15.760
But then, yes, beyond that, like, we all need
to get to the point where we can clearly see

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.039
that education inequities and mass criminalization
and anti-immigrant policies and all these

00:08:20.039 --> 00:08:25.050
other racial justice issues don't just harm
those who attend the under-resourced schools

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:30.430
or suffer the effects of overpolicing or or
face the prospect of being deported.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:37.189
If we examine our lives carefully, we will
all find evidence of harm caused by the reverberations

00:08:37.189 --> 00:08:39.349
from these unjust systems.

00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:43.899
And on the flip side, we also need to recognize
that people were fighting for racial justice,

00:08:43.899 --> 00:08:48.930
are trying to like we're often treated like
we're trying to ruin the world.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:53.720
But the reality is we're trying to build a
better world that benefits all people and

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:59.429
that when we do address these issues of systemic
racism, it doesn't just help people of color

00:08:59.429 --> 00:09:03.379
who have the burden of those racial inequities
lifted off them.

00:09:03.379 --> 00:09:08.879
We all stand to benefit when our neighbors
are able to live higher quality lives and

00:09:08.879 --> 00:09:11.499
really rot of injustice is purged from our
system.

00:09:11.499 --> 00:09:15.149
Speaker 1: What are some examples of where
you think the most notable progress has been

00:09:15.149 --> 00:09:18.209
made in the last 10 to 15 years on these issues?

00:09:18.209 --> 00:09:29.110
Speaker 3: Well, you know, I think the most
exciting progress happening within most recent

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:34.249
memory is is around law enforcement and the
criminal justice system and people are really

00:09:34.249 --> 00:09:42.740
starting to interrogate this issue of police
and the role that they serve and the role

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:43.740
that they should serve.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:44.740
Right.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:50.639
And people are asking questions, really important
questions about how we actually keep.

00:09:50.639 --> 00:09:59.569
Are our communities safe and healthy, and
why have we tasked so much the police departments

00:09:59.569 --> 00:10:03.660
with so much that really they're ill equipped
to handle?

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.300
I mean, that's certainly true in in black
and brown communities.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:08.970
But but it's true beyond that, too.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:16.360
I mean, I think we have to recognize how frankly,
how deeply we've lied to communities of color

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.120
over the year, made for 40 years.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:24.350
Basically, what US policymakers have been
saying to black and brown communities is,

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:28.949
is this we can we can best keep you safe by
being tough on crime, by flooding your communities

00:10:28.949 --> 00:10:33.769
with police officers, by using stop and frisk,
by employing broken windows, policing and

00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:38.689
cracking down on the level of offenses, by
putting police in schools, by locking up people

00:10:38.689 --> 00:10:43.610
at the highest rates anywhere in the world,
by doing the type of policing, frankly, that

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.750
killed George Floyd and Dante Wright and so
many others.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:50.410
And all along, the consistent message has
been that these were the most effective strategies

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.720
for improving public safety.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:54.360
But there is no evidence for that.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:58.519
You know, there are no heavily police to high
incarceration communities that are flourishing

00:10:58.519 --> 00:11:00.089
socially, economically culture.

00:11:00.089 --> 00:11:01.569
There are just none.

00:11:01.569 --> 00:11:04.379
It's a null set.

00:11:04.379 --> 00:11:10.480
So it really is a deep, deep lie that that
we've been telling those communities for many

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.839
years and folks are starting to see through
it.

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:16.369
And I think that's very exciting
Speaker 1: when you think about some of the

00:11:16.369 --> 00:11:20.480
decisions that are made by corporations that
are not directly involved.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:26.009
For example, look at incarceration, as you
talked about, or some of these other specific

00:11:26.009 --> 00:11:27.220
areas.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:34.970
It seems that there is a kind of vicious cycle
where even, you know, a chain restaurant based

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:40.889
on how it decides where to put new restaurants
and as a result, where jobs are created and

00:11:40.889 --> 00:11:45.259
what impact that has on the food choices of
people that may otherwise live in food deserts.

00:11:45.259 --> 00:11:50.859
I mean, a lot of these things seem very complex
to me because one can find reasons to think

00:11:50.859 --> 00:11:56.639
that almost any decision to give a really
specific example, think of a fast food chain

00:11:56.639 --> 00:12:02.680
that says, hey, you know what we do well in
poor neighborhoods that are relative food

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:07.009
deserts in terms of other options, there's
not that many grocery stores are healthier,

00:12:07.009 --> 00:12:09.189
healthier alternatives.

00:12:09.189 --> 00:12:10.990
And so that's where we're going to go.

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:14.799
And this worsens the health of the people
in the neighborhood by making OK.

00:12:14.799 --> 00:12:18.970
The counter, though, is, hey, they're creating
some jobs in that neighborhood, though.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:23.970
If every such business decided we're not going
to set up shop in this neighborhood, that's

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:24.970
100 hundred.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:25.970
That's two hundred.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.879
However many jobs that that aren't brought
there.

00:12:27.879 --> 00:12:30.060
And it very quickly becomes sort of like cloudy.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:34.829
How what is the what should be the top level
interpretation of some of these business decisions

00:12:34.829 --> 00:12:36.269
that relate to race?

00:12:36.269 --> 00:12:40.579
How do we disaggregate that and start to pick
apart something, something like that?

00:12:40.579 --> 00:12:46.699
Speaker 3: Yeah, I mean, I think.

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:54.199
I think it's certainly true that we have to
become much more disciplined as a country

00:12:54.199 --> 00:13:02.139
in analyzing all of those decisions and the
policies that influence those decisions through

00:13:02.139 --> 00:13:05.160
the lens of race, through the lens of systemic
racism.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:12.440
And I would suggest strategic racism, too,
because I'll go back to criminal justice,

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:20.119
because we were talking about it the last
summer when George Foy was killed, when he

00:13:20.119 --> 00:13:24.889
was murdered, we were told over and over again
that it was a wake up call for the nation.

00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:26.179
Right.

00:13:26.179 --> 00:13:27.910
But we never actually woke up.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:28.910
Right?

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:33.430
If we had, we would have started to have we
would have come together around real solutions

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:37.939
to those issues around the vastly oversize
and violent criminal justice system that's

00:13:37.939 --> 00:13:43.720
used within black communities as a catch all
solution for a huge variety of behaviors.

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:44.720
We didn't actually do that.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.540
We didn't even really start that conversation.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.549
But it's not because we don't know how.

00:13:50.549 --> 00:13:54.859
It's not because they don't have a fix like
we do.

00:13:54.859 --> 00:14:00.169
And there is we don't do it because there's
a very well-funded opposition that stands

00:14:00.169 --> 00:14:01.169
in the way.

00:14:01.169 --> 00:14:02.169
Right.

00:14:02.169 --> 00:14:07.689
So you have organizations like Alec representing
hundreds of the largest corporations in the

00:14:07.689 --> 00:14:11.679
US working for decades to pass those various
tough on crime policies.

00:14:11.679 --> 00:14:15.769
You have folks like the Koch brothers and
all these other billionaires who have been

00:14:15.769 --> 00:14:21.870
pouring tens of billions of dollars into various
right wing think tanks and advocacy organizations

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:23.920
that have been pushing this agenda and still
do.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.369
Today, you have one of those think tanks,
the Heritage Foundation, who after the uprising

00:14:28.369 --> 00:14:33.149
last summer against systemic racism, what
they decided to do is launch a back to blue

00:14:33.149 --> 00:14:37.060
campaign, a back to blue police pledge that
has now been signed by two hundred members

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:38.060
of Congress.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.959
So that's two hundred members of Congress
who have vowed to continue supporting the

00:14:40.959 --> 00:14:42.429
status quo in law enforcement.

00:14:42.429 --> 00:14:43.429
Right.

00:14:43.429 --> 00:14:47.279
And then one of those members of Congress,
Senator Tom Cotton, one of the most powerful

00:14:47.279 --> 00:14:50.470
Republicans in the country, who tweets out
a couple of weeks ago that the US, we have

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:53.529
a major under incarceration problem.

00:14:53.529 --> 00:14:59.549
So for me, that's what modern day racism looks
like, is that we implement these public policies

00:14:59.549 --> 00:15:03.919
that we know are going to inflict needless
harm on large groups of people that we know

00:15:03.919 --> 00:15:08.449
will perpetuate racial inequities that we
know will lead to people getting killed.

00:15:08.449 --> 00:15:12.959
And then when that harm becomes apparent,
we fail to address it appropriately, in significant

00:15:12.959 --> 00:15:16.420
part because it's economically beneficial
for the ultra wealthy.

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:18.369
So I think that's true in criminal justice.

00:15:18.369 --> 00:15:19.509
I think that's true in education.

00:15:19.509 --> 00:15:23.680
I think that's true and in public health.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:28.309
And so what we really need to do is if we're
going to address those issues, we really need

00:15:28.309 --> 00:15:33.629
to prioritize that and lift up those issues
and collectively start looking at these issues

00:15:33.629 --> 00:15:39.259
through the lens of race so that we can start
to design our public policies in a way that

00:15:39.259 --> 00:15:44.660
moves us towards the just and equitable society
that that all people deserve.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:51.660
Speaker 1: The book is Rich Thanks to Racism
How the Ultra Wealthy Profit from Racial Injustice.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.449
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Jim Freeman.

00:15:54.449 --> 00:15:55.929
Jim, so great having you on.

00:15:55.929 --> 00:15:57.399
And I really appreciate your time today.

00:15:57.399 --> 00:15:59.239
